 ## Background
-Reparameterization estimators originate from the broader field of differentiable programming and variational inference, where the "reparameterization trick" is used to estimate gradients of expectations with respect to distribution parameters [G]. In computer graphics, it was adapted to handle the non-differentiable nature of visibility and sampling in rendering. Key developments include the use of change of variables for silhouette gradients [S2], reparameterized volume sampling for neural radiance fields [S1], and parameter-space resampling techniques like ReSTIR for differentiable rendering [S1]. The method is widely used in inverse rendering, neural rendering, and differentiable physics simulation.
+Reparameterization estimators originate from the broader field of differentiable programming and stochastic gradient estimation, where the "reparameterization trick" is used to lower the variance of gradient estimates [S1]. In computer graphics, the application to rendering was driven by the need to handle visibility discontinuities (silhouettes and shadows) which cause non-differentiable jumps in the rendering integral when scene parameters change. Standard automatic differentiation fails or produces biased gradients in these cases because the domain of integration changes with the parameters [S1, S2, S3].
+
+The specific formulation for volume rendering and path tracing involves mapping parameter-dependent domains (such as the set of visible points or the sampling distribution induced by density) to fixed reference domains. This approach was notably applied to differentiable volume rendering to enable end-to-end training of neural radiance fields without auxiliary networks [S1] and to differentiable path tracing for implicit surfaces and complex materials [S2, S3]. The method is particularly relevant for inverse rendering tasks where geometry or material parameters must be optimized from images [S1, S2, S3].
 
